`guide_train()`, `guide_merge()`, `guide_geom()`, and `guide_gengrob()` were removed from the namespace of `ggplot2`'s development version I assume due to the guide system being ported to ggproto. These functions were added in version 3.0.0, and I would appreciate instead of quietly removing them in the new version, that these are kept in simply to throw an informative error.

for example instead of 

```r
guide_gengrob <- function(guide, theme) {
 UseMethod('guide_gengrob')
}
```

we have 

```r
guide_defunct <- function() {
  calling_fn <- parent.frame(2)
  calling_fn_env <- parent.env(calling_fn)

  rlang::abort(
    c(sprintf("`%s()` is defunct in favor of new ggproto `ggplot2::Guide` System",  deparse1(rlang::caller_call()[[1]])),
      'i' = sprintf('The package `%s` is using an old API which is not compatible with ggplot2 (v%s)',
                    calling_fn_env$.packageName,
                    packageVersion("ggplot2")),
      '*' = sprintf("Please contact the maintainer of `%s` to inform them of this change.",
                    calling_fn_env$.packageName)),
    call = calling_fn
  )
}

#' @export
guide_gengrob <- function(guide, theme) {
  guide_defunct()
}

#' @export
guide_train <- function(guide, scale, aesthetic = NULL) {
  guide_defunct()
}

#' @export
guide_merge <- function(guide, new_guide) {
  guide_defunct()
}

#' @export
guide_geom <- function(guide, layers, default_mapping) {
  guide_defunct()
}
```

It may be unlikely that the package that extends the old guide api will reach these functions first, but if they are called, then at least there is an informative message. 

Finally, in NEWS.md, the changes to these functions should be documented, even if these changes aren't implemented, we should at least state that they were removed.

